# SlayZone Env Vars — Standalone Hub & Runner

Every var surfaced by the full 4-cluster deep-dive, assessed for the standalone deploy surface. Dev/build/e2e/internal vars summarized at bottom.

**Precedence everywhere:** `env var > SLAYZONE_RUNNER_CONFIG file (runner) > ~/.slayzone/config.json > generated/default`

## Two orthogonal axes (why one abstraction isn't enough)

- **`SLAYZONE_SUPERVISED`** — *who owns the env*: Electron host injects everything (`=1`) vs process resolves its own. Governs **path anchoring, config.json reads, secret gen-vs-supplied**. → the two Default columns below.
- **`SLAYZONE_HOST` exposed** (non-loopback) — *is the client surface reachable off-box*. Governs **auth / network hardening**. → derived, NOT a column.

Three deploy shapes prove neither axis alone suffices:

| Shape | SUPERVISED | HOST exposed | paths from | auth |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Desktop app | yes | no (loopback) | host-injected | off |
| Fork sidecar | no | no (loopback) | own / CWD | off |
| Remote hub | no | yes | own / CWD | **on** |

Exposed-only can't split desktop↔fork (paths differ). Supervised-only can't split fork↔remote (auth differs). So: **supervised axis for paths, exposed axis for auth.** Deriving auth from *supervised* would break the standalone-but-loopback fork — do NOT.

## Single-anchor consolidation (`SLAYZONE_ROOT`)

Standalone collapses ALL path knobs into ONE optional var. `SLAYZONE_ROOT` (renamed from `SLAYZONE_HOME_DIR`) anchors BOTH hub + runner; everything derives. Default = **CWD** standalone, `$HOME/.slayzone` supervised.

```
SLAYZONE_ROOT  (default: CWD standalone)
├── slayzone.sqlite     ← was SLAYZONE_DB_PATH / SLAYZONE_STORE_DIR
├── config.json
├── logs/
└── runner/             ← creds (was SLAYZONE_RUNNER_CREDENTIALS_DIR)
```

Relocate everything = `cd /srv/slayzone && slayzone-hub` (or `SLAYZONE_ROOT=/srv/slayzone`).

**Minimize legend:** 🟢 keep · 🟠 hide (subsumed/advanced override, code stays) · 🔴 delete

